      "fixed_scope": "All 70 files match the refreshed exact scope hashes. The only changed formal content relative to the earlier 69-file scope is the source-index traceability addition; all previously reviewed gameplay, player wording, binding, and icon-exception conclusions remain unchanged.",
      "source_traceability": "The source index now links the actual Mk IV and Mk XIIIg sticky schedules, all selected sticky and final light/heavy profiles, ActionSweep per-instance Attack.execute routing, Attack ordinary on_hit skip gate, and the distinct special-hit event. The new links match the frozen sticky-routing addendum and its exact ranges.",
      "implementation": "The single chainsword_p1 implementation binds the actual Mk IV and Mk XIIIg. Own tiers .125/.15/.175/.20 override the wrapper .5 fallback; eligible held, same-item melee-special on_hit refreshes a nonstacking 4-second effect. Direct special-active light/heavy profiles may dispatch on_hit; ordinary push does not. The two Marks use their documented special push-follow profiles. Sticky profiles carry skip_on_hit_proc=true despite weapon_special=true, so attached/final ticks do not start or refresh this proc; their separate special-hit event is not the subscribed on_hit event.",
      "timing_and_math": "Proc processing and the subsequent player buff-stat-cache update occur before later movement calculations can read the effect; the triggering hit and previously computed movement are not retroactively changed. A qualifying hit refreshes the 4-second window without stacking. Switching away disables the held stat but does not pause expiry; switching back before expiry restores only the remaining window, while true unequip removes the source. The controlled Mk IV IV example uses V0=4.0 m/s, q=0, A=1 to obtain 4.8 m/s after cache update; a t=13 hit inside t<14 refreshes to t<17 at +20%, not +40%.",
      "raw_and_identity": "The paired EN/ZH RAW name/description keys and hashes are preserved, and I–IV reconstruction matches the formatter values and 4-second wrapper duration. The qualified document identity distinguishes this melee entry from the already-existing ranged Hit & Run without changing the raw name, key, or binding.",
